{:edb/db-before-t 645 :edb/db-after-t 646 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533958 50 #inst "2026-10-05T17:31:38.890Z" 13194139533958 true] [17592186110511 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268781 13194139533958 true] [17592186110511 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268788 13194139533958 true] [17592186110511 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268795 13194139533958 true] [17592186110511 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268844 13194139533958 true] [17592186110511 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268865 13194139533958 true] [17592186110512 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268816 13194139533958 true] [17592186110512 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268823 13194139533958 true] [17592186110512 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268837 13194139533958 true] [17592186110512 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268886 13194139533958 true] [17592186110512 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268893 13194139533958 true] [17592186110513 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268802 13194139533958 true] [17592186110513 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268809 13194139533958 true] [17592186110513 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268872 13194139533958 true] [17592186110513 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268879 13194139533958 true] [17592186110514 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268830 13194139533958 true] [17592186110514 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268851 13194139533958 true] [17592186110514 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268858 13194139533958 true] [17592186110514 1020 #edb/ref 17592186268900 13194139533958 true] [17592186111097 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533958 true] [17592186111097 1145 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, all the given values are in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" 13194139533958 true] [17592186111289 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533958 true] [17592186111289 1145 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268781 1017 #edb/ref 17592186268782 13194139533958 true] [17592186268781 1089 "q-117263" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268781 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533958 true] [17592186268781 1100 "What is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268781 1113 #uuid "d05a2d90-8420-53be-8f32-dc634d54e85e" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268781 1145 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain is all real numbers.\n\n- There is a zero at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is negative over $(-\\infty ,0)$ and positive over $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing in its entire domain.\n\n- $y\\to -\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from above) as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- $y\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from below) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The lines $y=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are horizontal asymptotes of the function.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268783 13194139533958 true] [17592186268782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268784 13194139533958 true] [17592186268782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268785 13194139533958 true] [17592186268782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268786 13194139533958 true] [17592186268782 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268787 13194139533958 true] [17592186268782 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533958 true] [17592186268782 1041 "selection" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268782 1104 #edb/ref 17592186268785 13194139533958 true] [17592186268782 1110 #uuid "5e5cf231-7e9e-5ec9-aa40-68c62eecc0a8" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268783 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/5acaaf6c4825f67fd5e4acb95b3d85068e9a030de4502d8baa5dd1dcd7888ea9.png" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268783 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533958 true] [17592186268783 1111 #uuid "c21b6f60-3712-518e-9853-e56afb9aa36b" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268784 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268784 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533958 true] [17592186268784 1111 #uuid "46f14eef-7d6d-52f6-933c-caa1682b0e2