rue] [17592186268828 1111 #uuid "422283c5-10c5-53b0-8c47-384b2c7012fb" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268829 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268829 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533958 true] [17592186268829 1111 #uuid "81050128-e7e0-52d1-b2cd-c131cd64e391" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268830 1017 #edb/ref 17592186268831 13194139533958 true] [17592186268830 1089 "q-117369" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268830 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533958 true] [17592186268830 1100 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268830 1113 #uuid "d42d29a1-7ec6-555e-8ef7-3216eb563375" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268830 1145 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268832 13194139533958 true] [17592186268831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268833 13194139533958 true] [17592186268831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268834 13194139533958 true] [17592186268831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268835 13194139533958 true] [17592186268831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186268836 13194139533958 true] [17592186268831 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533958 true] [17592186268831 1041 "selection" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268831 1104 #edb/ref 17592186268836 13194139533958 true] [17592186268831 1110 #uuid "acdc22b1-47e7-5a65-9469-46650f645be9" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268832 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268832 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533958 true] [17592186268832 1111 #uuid "03a59404-b27c-5964-ba25-9e75a1e95007" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268833 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268833 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533958 true] [17592186268833 1111 #uuid "1af021bc-ba81-5212-8e00-8e06543db63b" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268834 1034 "I only" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268834 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533958 true] [17592186268834 1111 #uuid "c908c8e3-8fed-5d51-9a52-edb7a0beb40c" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268835 1034 "III only" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268835 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533958 true] [17592186268835 1111 #uuid "9dfbf96c-d27c-5c92-b142-d4169db645a0" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268836 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533958 true] [17592186268836 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533958 true] 